{
  "machine": "static_host_cose",
  "about": "Signing and sealing, random bytes, keyed digests, keys and credentials of docs/SCE_HOST_FEATURES.md, sections 10 to 14, as a machine sees them through its host. `host_state.credentials` is each name the host issued a token for, to its tokens as `issued_ms`, `expires_ms` and `revoked`, in that order: a token's own bytes are the tag of the session's identity, which differs by lane, and are not stated. A `cose_keys` directive replaces the keys the host is configured with: `sign` maps a key id to the hexadecimal 32-byte seed of an Ed25519 key, `aead` a key id to a hexadecimal 32-byte ChaCha20/Poly1305 key, `mac` a key id to a hexadecimal HMAC-SHA-256 key of any length, and `trust` a trust set's name to the ids of the signing keys whose public keys it holds; the keys the host generated and derived are kept. A `random` directive gives the hexadecimal bytes the host's random source gives next, before the operating system's: a draw, a generation (the id's 16 bytes, then the key's 32), the naming key of the store's first derivation (32 bytes) and a seal's nonce take them in the order the host draws. `jefe` and `rfc4231-1` are the keys of RFC 4231 test cases 2 and 1; the generated keys and their COSE_Key are those of tests/host_features/keys_cross_language.json. Byte strings travel as their byte-exact Latin-1 text. `11` is the key of RFC 8032 section 7.1 test 1 and of the COSE working group's EdDSA examples, `other` that of test 2, `sec-256` the key of the working group's ChaCha20/Poly1305 examples. The objects of a host's own signatures and of the objects sealed elsewhere were computed by an implementation that shares nothing with any reference host, which first reproduced the working group's signature of eddsa-sig-01.json. An `audit` directive replaces the streams the host keeps (`streams`, each a name to its `checkpoint_every`, its `retain_days` and, optionally, `personal`: `pseudonymised` or `clear`); `host_state.audit` is, for each stream it names, its rows as `sequence`, `at_ms` and `entry`, the sequences of its checkpoints and whether a reader verifies it under the host's audit key (section 15); the entries and their keyed digests are those of tests/host_features/audit_cross_language.json, whose pseudonymisation key and audit seed the `random` directive gives in the order the host draws them. Expected values are derived from the specification, not observed from a host.",
